==================================================================
DAY 47 - PART B: LAB
Give a pod S3 access without any keys
Time: 90 minutes
==================================================================

STEP 1: Cluster and a bucket
----------------------------
cd ~/k8s-journey/cloud/eks && eksctl create cluster -f cluster.yaml
ACCOUNT=$(aws sts get-caller-identity --query Account --output text)
BUCKET=k8s-lab-$ACCOUNT-$RANDOM ; echo $BUCKET
aws s3 mb s3://$BUCKET --region ap-south-1
echo "hello from s3" > hello.txt && aws s3 cp hello.txt s3://$BUCKET/


STEP 2: A pod WITHOUT permissions
---------------------------------
k create ns demo
k run nocreds -n demo --image=amazon/aws-cli --command -- sleep 3600
k exec -n demo nocreds -- aws sts get-caller-identity
k exec -n demo nocreds -- aws s3 ls s3://$BUCKET
Note: it may show the NODE role identity (via instance metadata) and be
denied for S3. Production clusters also block pod access to instance
metadata (IMDS hop limit 1 / IMDSv2) so pods can't borrow node
credentials.


STEP 3: IAM policy scoped to ONE bucket
---------------------------------------
cat > s3-read-policy.json <<EOF
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {"Effect": "Allow", "Action": ["s3:ListBucket"], "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::$BUCKET"},
    {"Effect": "Allow", "Action": ["s3:GetObject"], "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::$BUCKET/*"}
  ]
}
EOF
aws iam create-policy --policy-name lab-s3-read --policy-document file://s3-read-policy.json


STEP 4: EKS Pod Identity association
------------------------------------
k create sa s3-reader -n demo
eksctl create podidentityassociation --cluster lab --namespace demo \
  --service-account-name s3-reader \
  --permission-policy-arns arn:aws:iam::$ACCOUNT:policy/lab-s3-read
eksctl get podidentityassociation --cluster lab
(eksctl creates an IAM role trusted by pods.eks.amazonaws.com.)


STEP 5: A pod WITH the ServiceAccount
-------------------------------------
k run reader -n demo --image=amazon/aws-cli \
  --overrides='{"spec":{"serviceAccountName":"s3-reader"}}' --command -- sleep 3600
k exec -n demo reader -- env | grep AWS_
k exec -n demo reader -- aws sts get-caller-identity       # the new role!
k exec -n demo reader -- aws s3 ls s3://$BUCKET
k exec -n demo reader -- aws s3 cp s3://$BUCKET/hello.txt -
k exec -n demo reader -- sh -c "echo x > /tmp/x && aws s3 cp /tmp/x s3://$BUCKET/x"   # AccessDenied (read-only)
k exec -n demo reader -- aws s3 ls                           # AccessDenied (other buckets)

No keys anywhere: credentials are temporary and rotate automatically.


STEP 6 (COMPARE): IRSA
----------------------
eksctl create iamserviceaccount --cluster lab --namespace demo --name s3-reader-irsa \
  --attach-policy-arn arn:aws:iam::$ACCOUNT:policy/lab-s3-read --approve
k get sa s3-reader-irsa -n demo -o yaml | grep role-arn
k run irsa -n demo --image=amazon/aws-cli \
  --overrides='{"spec":{"serviceAccountName":"s3-reader-irsa"}}' --command -- sleep 3600
k exec -n demo irsa -- env | grep -E "AWS_ROLE_ARN|AWS_WEB_IDENTITY_TOKEN_FILE"
k exec -n demo irsa -- aws s3 ls s3://$BUCKET
Look at the IAM role's trust policy in the console: it trusts the OIDC
provider and the exact SA subject.


STEP 7 (OPTIONAL): External Secrets Operator
--------------------------------------------
aws secretsmanager create-secret --name lab/db --secret-string '{"password":"fromAWS"}'
helm repo add external-secrets https://charts.external-secrets.io
helm install external-secrets external-secrets/external-secrets -n external-secrets --create-namespace
Create a policy allowing secretsmanager:GetSecretValue on that secret,
associate it with a ServiceAccount "eso" in demo (Step 4 method), then:

apiVersion: external-secrets.io/v1
kind: SecretStore
metadata: {name: aws, namespace: demo}
spec:
  provider:
    aws:
      service: SecretsManager
      region: ap-south-1
      auth:
        jwt:
          serviceAccountRef: {name: eso}
---
apiVersion: external-secrets.io/v1
kind: ExternalSecret
metadata: {name: db, namespace: demo}
spec:
  refreshInterval: 1m
  secretStoreRef: {name: aws, kind: SecretStore}
  target: {name: db-secret}
  data:
  - secretKey: password
    remoteRef: {key: lab/db, property: password}

(If your installed version only serves v1beta1, change apiVersion.
With Pod Identity the jwt auth block may be omitted; follow the ESO
AWS docs for your version.)
k get externalsecret -n demo ; k get secret db-secret -n demo -o jsonpath='{.data.password}' | base64 -d
aws secretsmanager delete-secret --secret-id lab/db --force-delete-without-recovery


STEP 8: CLEAN UP
----------------
aws s3 rb s3://$BUCKET --force
eksctl delete podidentityassociation --cluster lab --namespace demo --service-account-name s3-reader
eksctl delete iamserviceaccount --cluster lab --namespace demo --name s3-reader-irsa
eksctl delete cluster -f ~/k8s-journey/cloud/eks/cluster.yaml --wait
aws iam delete-policy --policy-arn arn:aws:iam::$ACCOUNT:policy/lab-s3-read

cd ~/k8s-journey && git add . && git commit -m "day47: workload identity"
